/-!
# Rational Chow-correspondence realization

This umbrella module collects the noncommutative Chow-correspondence model,
its presentation by two-sided relation ideals, and its finite-cover/Galois
projector realization theorem, the Tate-block graph-projector reduction
connecting algebraic projector lifts to the modeled rational Hodge
cycle-class target, the rational-linear
comparison from the marked Galois residual block to that graph operator, and
the universal one-idempotent representation morphism, and the conditional
lower-left and surjective-cancellation endpoints for a
chosen correspondence, with extension-based selector comparisons retained
as stronger specializations. The endpoint
uses the augmentation ideal of the commutative sheet algebra and keeps the
residual cohomological realization linear.  Its universal-algebra formulation
exposes the projector homomorphisms without requiring a map from the whole
pointwise sheet algebra to the convolution algebra, or a restriction in the
reverse direction. It still requires an
explicit geometric residual realization and action square; it constructs
neither for a general variety. For split homogeneous sheets, the actual
residual ideal now maps bijectively to point-sum Chow classes and preserves
intersection. Diagonal pushforward and an invertible Chow shear construct
the sheet-to-correspondence algebra map; `Realization.Betti` proves its
marked action comparison. Multiplicative residual rigidity forces the
generated class to be minus the unit when the residual map is surjective.
The auxiliary linear coset-average comparison is archived under
`research/archive/legacy-lean/`: pointwise and convolution products differ,
and this map is not an active geometric bridge.

The split-span realization now connects actual split-presentable geometric
classes to rational matrices by a surjective algebra map. The two-sided ideal
generated by actual finite point-decomposition relations is exactly its kernel,
so the additive quotient is isomorphic to the matrix algebra. The two-point
comparison and target square therefore have an actual scheme-span source, and
the action and target square descend to this quotient. Separately, cycles on
actual integral closed subschemes and the geometric no-curves theorem compute
the finite split Chow group as point coordinates, over both integers and
rationals; the rational group is canonically the scalar extension of the
integral one. Actual product and fiber comparisons, image residue degrees,
and fiber stalk lengths now construct the geometric cycle operations.
Pull--intersection--push gives matrix multiplication, with the diagonal as
unit. The resulting Chow convolution algebra is isomorphic to the additive
span quotient, sending each span to its carrier's proper cycle pushforward.
Its actual Chow action is matrix-vector multiplication in point coordinates.
`Realization.Betti` constructs the singular degree-zero cycle-class
comparison and cohomological action for these schemes over the complex field.
No realization on all nonsplit finite-étale classes is claimed.

The upstairs-generation criterion fixes a cycle recipe and asks it to
represent the pullbacks of the independently specified downstairs Hodge
classes. An indexed version allows the chosen recipe to vary with the class.
Existing positive-degree transfer data and explicit cycle-class/pushforward
compatibility give the normalized representative downstairs. This conditional
criterion needs no projector, deck action, or surjective residual map;
constructing the geometric maps and proving generation remain separate tasks.

Boundary correction uses the actual rational Chow restriction and closed
pushforward: an open-locus representative gives an ambient representative
under explicit cycle-class comparison squares, a supported Hodge lift, and
algebraicity on the boundary. The latter can be a lower-codimension induction
hypothesis. General Betti/Gysin maps and the open-locus construction remain
separate geometric obligations.

The marked cycle map assembles actual homogeneous cycles on an arbitrary
ambient scheme using residual sheet coefficients, then takes their existing
rational Chow class. Its generated class and exact image are computed, and
the map commutes with actual open restriction and finite pushforward. The
family of cycles is geometric input; selecting one for a prescribed Hodge
class and constructing the general Betti/action comparisons remain open.

The superseded commutative selector-generated specialization is also
archived outside the library, not available through its former import path.

Supported-class extraction expands a supplied cohomological component basis
into actual homogeneous cycles on a closed scheme and pushes them into the
ambient Chow group. Its comparison and vanishing criterion are proved from
explicit cohomological exactness and fundamental-class comparisons. The
geometric supported-cohomology basis and construction of a support for each
Hodge class remain open; the extraction introduces no new object types.
-/
